// src/kernel/core/cap/kind_gpio_line.pdx — R30.M6-001 (#1075)
//
// KIND_GPIO_LINE derived kind: authority over ONE pin on ONE pad
// controller.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE PROPERTY THIS FILE EXISTS TO MAKE TRUE
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
//   A holder of a line capability can act on the pin its capability
//   names, and cannot act on any other pin on the package.
//
// This is `KIND_I2C_SLAVE`'s property with the stakes raised, and the
// raise is worth stating precisely rather than gesturing at.
//
// On I²C, addressing the wrong device produces a data error: the wrong
// peripheral answers, or none does, and a driver reads nonsense. On a
// pad controller, acting on the wrong pin produces a PHYSICAL STATE
// CHANGE with no error path at all. The pins a real pad controller owns
// include device reset asserts, power-rail enables, write-protect
// straps and firmware-flash control. There is no NACK, no arbitration
// loss, no status bit that says "that was the wrong pin". The pin simply
// becomes whatever the last writer made it, and the first symptom
// appears somewhere else entirely — a device that never came back from
// reset, a rail that is off, a strap that no longer protects anything.
//
// So the mechanism is the same sentence, and it is enforced harder:
//
//   THE PIN COMES FROM THE CAPABILITY ROW, NEVER FROM THE CALLER.
//
// `gpio_line_pin_of_slot(slot)` takes no pin argument. Neither does
// `gpio_line_pad_of_slot`, `gpio_line_community_of_slot`,
// `gpio_line_ctrl_of_slot`, or `gpio_line_pad_off_of_slot` — the
// function that produces the register address R30.M6-004 (#1078) will
// drive. None of them has anywhere to put a caller-supplied pin,
// community or pad index, and `tools/build.sh` pins the literal
// signature of every one of them under the `gpio-pin-confine` step, so a
// mutant that adds a pin parameter fails the build rather than the
// review.
//
// FOUR resolvers are pinned rather than one, and that is not
// belt-and-braces. The register address of a pad is
//
//     community.reg_off + community.padbar + pad_index * stride
//
// so a caller-supplied COMMUNITY reaches a different pin exactly as
// surely as a caller-supplied pin does, and a caller-supplied PAD INDEX
// more surely still. A confinement that guarded only the pin number
// would leave two doors into the same room. This is the same argument
// #1073 made when it pinned `i2c_slave_mode_of_slot` and
// `i2c_slave_bus_row_of_slot` alongside the address resolver: every
// field that participates in selecting the physical target is part of
// the target.
//
// The pin IS an argument at MINT, and that is the only place it can be.
// Minting is the authorising moment: it is gated on the parent
// KIND_DEVICE capability with RIGHT_MINT, that device must be a PROBED
// PAD CONTROLLER, and the platform is deciding there that this driver
// owns this pin. After that the row is the only source of truth.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE COMMUNITY IS DERIVED, NOT SUPPLIED — AND THAT IS THE POINT
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// `gpio_line_cap_mint` takes a PIN and nothing else about location. The
// community and the community-relative pad index are computed by
// `lpss_gpio_resolve_pin`, stored in the row, and never recomputed.
//
// Intel pad controllers organise pads into communities, each with its
// own register window and its own pad-group base. The ACPI-visible pin
// number is ABSOLUTE across the controller; the index that addresses a
// pad's registers is COMMUNITY-RELATIVE:
//
//     community  = the c with c.pin_base <= pin < c.pin_base + c.npins
//     pad_index  = pin - c.pin_base
//
// For the first community `pin_base` is 0 and the mapping is the
// identity. It is the identity NOWHERE ELSE. A driver that skips the
// subtraction is therefore correct on every pin of community 0 and
// drives the wrong pin on every pin of every other community, silently.
//
// Making the caller supply the community would put that arithmetic —
// the arithmetic whose failure mode is "a different pin than intended,
// with no error" — in every caller. Deriving it once, at the one moment
// authority is granted, from the one table that knows the geometry, is
// what removes the class of bug rather than relocating it.
//
// That is also why this module calls into `core/drivers/gpio/`:
// `lpss_gpio_resolve_pin` lives with the controller table because the
// mapping is a property of the controller. Duplicating the geometry into
// the capability module would put it in two places, and the failure mode
// of the two disagreeing is a capability that records one pad while the
// driver drives another — precisely the accident this kind exists to
// prevent. The dependency is one-directional and one function wide.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT A LINE CAPABILITY DOES **NOT** YET PERMIT
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// R30.M6-001 (#1075) shipped capability STRUCTURE only. R30.M6-003
// (#1077) added the CHANNEL and R30.M6-004 (#1078) the driver beneath
// it. Holding a KIND_GPIO_LINE now permits, per right:
//
//   RIGHT_INVOKE   reading back the row's own pin, community, pad index,
//                  controller identity, driver hint and cached
//                  configuration byte;
//   RIGHT_READ     sampling the pin's level;
//   RIGHT_WRITE    driving a pin THIS KERNEL ALREADY MADE AN OUTPUT;
//   RIGHT_CONFIG   direction, pull, and edge subscribe / unsubscribe;
//   RIGHT_OBSERVE  the canonical debug dump and the serviced-edge count;
//   RIGHT_REVOKE   being revoked.
//
// It still does NOT permit, and there is no code that would honour:
//
//   * deriving anything. This kind is a LEAF of the derivation lattice
//     and carries no MINT right at all;
//   * anything about any OTHER pin. A line holder cannot enumerate the
//     controller's communities, cannot count its siblings, and cannot
//     learn how many pins the part has;
//   * naming a pin, a community or a pad index anywhere. The channel ops
//     carry an eight-bit operand and refuse an invocation with anything
//     set above it, and every pad address still comes from
//     gpio_line_pad_off_of_slot(slot).
//
// THE RESERVATION HELD, AND THAT IS THE POINT OF HAVING MADE IT.
// RIGHT_READ, RIGHT_WRITE and R_GPIO_LINE_CONFIG were placed inside
// R_GPIO_LINE_ALL by #1075 with nothing consuming them, for the reason
// #1071 gave: a capability minted then with rights 0x418 is refused a
// level change now, because the op demands bits that capability was
// never given. Had the bits been added with the ops instead, every
// already-minted line would either have needed re-minting or would have
// silently acquired the authority to drive a pin on the day the op
// appeared. The witness in tests/kernel/cap/gpio_cap_synth.pdx §K
// asserts exactly that: INVOKE alone -- the whole authority a query-only
// holder was minted with -- still reaches no channel op.
//
// CONFIG IS A SEPARATE BIT FROM WRITE, and on this kind that separation
// carries more weight than READ/WRITE does. Driving an output that
// firmware already configured as an output is an ordinary operation.
// Turning an INPUT into an output is not: a pin that firmware left as an
// input may be a strap another device is driving, and a driver that
// reconfigures it to an output starts a contention on a physical net.
// "May toggle this line, may not change what kind of line it is" has to
// be expressible, and folding CONFIG into WRITE would make it
// inexpressible.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// PIN VALIDITY — refused per reason, never clamped
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
//   pin >= GPIO_PIN_MAX (512)     -> GPIO_LINE_MINT_PIN_RANGE
//   no community covers the pin   -> GPIO_LINE_MINT_PIN_UNMAPPED
//   the parent names no probed
//     pad controller              -> GPIO_LINE_MINT_NO_CONTROLLER
//
// Three codes, because they are three different operator problems: a pin
// number that cannot be represented, a pin the platform did not route on
// this part, and a device capability for something that is not a pad
// controller at all. Collapsing them would make a driver bound to the
// wrong PCI function look like a driver asking for a pin the board does
// not have.
//
// REFUSAL, NEVER CLAMPING. This deserves saying explicitly because
// clamping is the tempting repair: an out-of-range pin has an obvious
// nearest legal value, and returning it keeps the boot going. It also
// hands the caller SOME pin — and on this part some pin is a reset line
// or a rail enable. A capability that names a pin nobody asked for is
// strictly worse than no capability, because the driver will use it.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// PIN COLLISION — the second mint is REFUSED
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// Two live line capabilities for the same (controller, pin) are refused
// with GPIO_LINE_MINT_PIN_IN_USE. The uniqueness key is
// (controller_id, pin) — SCOPED TO THE CONTROLLER, not global.
//
// WHY REFUSE. The I²C argument for refusing a duplicate address was that
// two owners interleaving register-pointer-then-read sequences silently
// read each other's data. The GPIO argument is stronger, and it is
// stronger in kind rather than in degree.
//
// A pin has ONE STATE. It is not a transaction that can be serialised,
// retried, or arbitrated; it is a voltage on a net. Two drivers each
// believing they own a reset line do not race for a resource — one holds
// the device in reset and the other releases it, and which one wins is
// whichever wrote last. Neither can detect the other: there is no
// protocol on a pin, no acknowledgement, no error. And unlike a bus
// transaction, the loser's failure does not surface in the loser's
// driver; it surfaces in whatever hardware the pin controls.
//
// There is also no arbiter shape that fixes it after the fact. On I²C a
// device that genuinely must be shared can be fronted by one holder that
// serialises access, because the underlying operation is a transaction
// with a beginning and an end. A pin's state has no end. The only
// coherent way to share one is for exactly one component to own it and
// expose its own interface, which is a thing you build — and building it
// starts with the capability being unique.
//
// So permitting the second mint would make the broken configuration the
// easy one, on the class of hardware where the broken configuration is
// least recoverable.
//
// WHY THE KEY IS SCOPED TO THE CONTROLLER. A machine with two pad
// controllers numbers each one's pins from zero. Pin 5 on the PCH
// controller and pin 5 on a second controller are two different pieces
// of silicon, and there is no sense in which they conflict. A global pin
// table would refuse the second, which is not a conservative failure —
// it would make an ordinary two-controller board unbootable.
//
// WHY THE KEY IS THE ABSOLUTE PIN AND NOT (community, pad_index). They
// are equivalent — the mapping is a bijection within a controller — but
// the absolute pin is the number the platform description actually
// carries, so keying on it means the uniqueness test is applied to the
// number the caller supplied rather than to a derived one. It also makes
// the following true and testable: THE SAME PAD INDEX IN TWO DIFFERENT
// COMMUNITIES COEXISTS. Community 0 pad 5 and community 1 pad 5 are
// different absolute pins on different register windows, and a
// uniqueness key that looked only at the pad index would wrongly refuse
// the second.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// DERIVATION — over KIND_DEVICE, and the two-part gate
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// The gate demands `kind == KIND_DEVICE (10)` carrying RIGHT_MINT, and
// then it demands something the kind check cannot express: that the
// device the capability names is a PROBED PAD CONTROLLER. The controller
// identity is read from the parent descriptor's `target_ptr` — inherited,
// never argued, the same discipline `i2c_bus_parent_controller_id`
// established — and looked up in `_lpss_gpio_ctrl`. A KIND_DEVICE
// capability for the network card passes the kind check and is refused
// GPIO_LINE_MINT_NO_CONTROLLER.
//
// That second half is what makes the gate mean something. A pad
// controller is not distinguishable from any other PCI function by its
// capability kind, so a gate that only checked the kind would let any
// device-capability holder mint pins on the pad controller merely by
// naming its identity — except that it cannot name it, because the
// identity is inherited. The lookup is the check that the inheritance
// actually landed on a pad controller.
//
// Revoking the controller CASCADES to every line derived from it:
// `lpss_gpio_release` calls `gpio_line_cascade_revoke_by_controller`
// BEFORE it revokes the window or frees its own row. A line that
// outlived its controller would be worse than an I²C slave that outlived
// its bus, because a controller row can be reallocated by a later probe
// and the surviving capability's recorded controller_id would then name
// DIFFERENT SILICON.
//
// The cascade has TWO PHASES and both are necessary:
//
//   Phase 1 sweeps by DESCRIPTOR — every cap_table slot holding a
//   KIND_GPIO_LINE whose row records this controller.
//
//   Phase 2 sweeps by ROW — every live row recording this controller,
//   even if no descriptor points at it any more. Phase 2 is what makes
//   "no row leak" structural rather than probable: a descriptor cleared
//   by some other path would otherwise strand its row forever, and a
//   stranded row still holds a PIN on a controller that no longer
//   exists, so the next mint of that pin would be refused IN_USE by a
//   ghost.
//
// A single level suffices. KIND_GPIO_LINE is a leaf — it carries no MINT
// right — so there are no grandchildren and no fixed point to reach.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// DESCRIPTOR TAIL — row indirection (the R29/R30 family shape)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// Row layout — `_gpio_line_table[row_id]`, 32 bytes (four u64s):
//
//   [+0]  u64:  bits [7:0]   = parent_slot   (cap_table slot of the
//                                             KIND_DEVICE at mint time;
//                                             recorded for audit and
//                                             query, NOT the cascade key)
//               bits [23:8]  = pin           (u16; ABSOLUTE pin number
//                                             on the controller,
//                                             < GPIO_PIN_MAX)
//               bits [39:24] = pad_index     (u16; COMMUNITY-RELATIVE,
//                                             derived at mint and never
//                                             recomputed)
//               bits [47:40] = community     (u8)
//               bits [55:48] = reserved (must be zero) — R30.M6-004's
//                                             direction / pull / trigger
//                                             configuration
//               bits [63:56] = in_use        (0 = free, 1 = allocated)
//   [+8]  u64:  controller_id — the bdf_pack key INHERITED from the
//               parent KIND_DEVICE descriptor's target_ptr. The true
//               parent identity: the uniqueness key's first term and the
//               cascade key, because it names the PART rather than a cap
//               slot that could later hold a different device.
//   [+16] u64:  driver_hint — opaque owner identity, non-zero. Zero is
//               refused at allocation: zero is the "unclaimed" sentinel
//               the R31 binder reads, and a capability that is both
//               minted and unclaimed is a state with no meaning.
//   [+24] u64:  reserved (must be zero) — R30.M6-004's edge-interrupt
//               subscription state.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// RIGHTS
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
//   R_GPIO_LINE_READ    (0x001) — reserved for #1077's level get
//   R_GPIO_LINE_WRITE   (0x002) — reserved for #1077's level set
//   R_GPIO_LINE_CONFIG  (0x004) — reserved for #1078's direction / pull
//                                 / trigger; SEPARATE from WRITE on
//                                 purpose, see above
//   R_GPIO_LINE_INVOKE  (0x008) — the query ops
//   R_GPIO_LINE_REVOKE  (0x010) — teardown
//   R_GPIO_LINE_OBSERVE (0x400) — the canonical printer
//   R_GPIO_LINE_ALL     (0x41F) — bitwise OR of the six.
//
// RIGHT_MINT (0x200) IS ABSENT AND ITS ABSENCE IS STRUCTURAL. There is
// nothing below a single pin on a single controller. A MINT bit here
// would be a bit no gate reads, which is the shape that later acquires a
// meaning nobody audited.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// TABLE SIZING
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// GPIO_LINE_MAX = 32. A T14 G4 routes on the order of a dozen pins to
// things a driver owns — touchpad interrupt, touchscreen reset,
// fingerprint reader enable, camera privacy, a handful of power
// enables — and 32 leaves headroom without making the linear scans
// meaningful work. Storage: 32 * 32 = 1024 bytes .bss, align 64.
//
// `tools/build.sh` asserts with `objdump -r` that no kernel object other
// than kind_gpio_line.o relocates against `_gpio_line_table`. That is
// what makes "the pin lives in the row and the row is reachable only
// through this module's functions" a property of the kernel rather than
// of one file.

module KindGpioLine = structure {

  // ==========================================================================
  // Kind tags
  //
  // 0x154 is the fifth entry in the R30 derived-kind block, after
  // KIND_OP_REGION (0x150), KIND_ACPI_EVENT (0x151), KIND_I2C_BUS
  // (0x152) and KIND_I2C_SLAVE (0x153). Above the 4-bit base-slot range,
  // so the LAM kind-hint is unaffected.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let KIND_GPIO_LINE : u64 = 0x154   // derived-kind tag (decimal 340)

  // The parent kind. A BASE slot, not a derived tag: unlike an I²C
  // slave, whose parent bus is itself a derived capability proving the
  // controller was brought up at a validated rate, a pad controller has
  // no intermediate "the pads are configured" authority to derive over.
  // What stands in for it is the SECOND half of the gate — the parent's
  // inherited identity must name a probed, identified pad controller —
  // which is a check on the machine rather than on a capability, and is
  // the honest way to express "this device really is one".
  pub let GPIO_LINE_PARENT_KIND : u64 = 10   // = KIND_DEVICE

  // ==========================================================================
  // Rights
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let R_GPIO_LINE_READ    : u64 = 0x001
  pub let R_GPIO_LINE_WRITE   : u64 = 0x002
  pub let R_GPIO_LINE_CONFIG  : u64 = 0x004
  pub let R_GPIO_LINE_INVOKE  : u64 = 0x008
  pub let R_GPIO_LINE_REVOKE  : u64 = 0x010
  pub let R_GPIO_LINE_OBSERVE : u64 = 0x400
  pub let R_GPIO_LINE_ALL     : u64 = 0x41F

  // ==========================================================================
  // Table caps
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let GPIO_LINE_MAX       : u64 = 32
  pub let GPIO_LINE_ROW_BYTES : u64 = 32

  // ==========================================================================
  // Return codes
  //
  // Block 0xFFFFFF10..0xFFFFFF1F — sixteen codes, exactly the width of
  // the band, and disjoint from GPIO_IO_* (0xFFFFFF08..0F) directly
  // below, LPSS_GPIO_* (0xFFFFFF20..2F) directly above, DW_IO_*
  // (0xFFFFFF30..3F), LPSS_I2C_* (0xFFFFFF40..5F), I2C_XFER_*
  // (0xFFFFFF60..6F), I2C_SLAVE_* (0xFFFFFF70..7F), I2C_BUS_*
  // (0xFFFFFF80..8F), ACPI_EVT_* (0xFFFFFF90..9F), DRV_RESTART_*
  // (0xFFFFFFB5..BE), OPREG_* (0xFFFFFFC2..CF), DMA_* (0xFFFFFFD5..DF),
  // MSIX_* (0xFFFFFFE7..EF) and HW_INT_* (0xFFFFFFF7..FF).
  //
  // Three neighbouring bands (seam, capability, driver) matters more
  // here than usual: all three refuse for structurally similar reasons
  // during a probe, and a shared band would make "which layer refused"
  // a guess.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let GPIO_LINE_OK                  : u64 = 0
  pub let GPIO_LINE_TAIL_ENOSPC         : u64 = 0xFFFFFF1F
  pub let GPIO_LINE_TAIL_BAD_ARG        : u64 = 0xFFFFFF1E
  pub let GPIO_LINE_MINT_BAD_PARENT     : u64 = 0xFFFFFF1D
  pub let GPIO_LINE_MINT_BAD_RIGHTS     : u64 = 0xFFFFFF1C
  pub let GPIO_LINE_MINT_BAD_ARG        : u64 = 0xFFFFFF1B
  pub let GPIO_LINE_MINT_ENOSPC         : u64 = 0xFFFFFF1A
  pub let GPIO_LINE_MINT_PIN_RANGE      : u64 = 0xFFFFFF19
  pub let GPIO_LINE_MINT_PIN_UNMAPPED   : u64 = 0xFFFFFF18
  pub let GPIO_LINE_MINT_PIN_IN_USE     : u64 = 0xFFFFFF17
  pub let GPIO_LINE_MINT_NO_CONTROLLER  : u64 = 0xFFFFFF16
  pub let GPIO_LINE_MINT_BAD_HINT       : u64 = 0xFFFFFF15
  pub let GPIO_LINE_REVOKE_BAD_SLOT     : u64 = 0xFFFFFF14
  pub let GPIO_LINE_REVOKE_WRONG_KIND   : u64 = 0xFFFFFF13
  pub let GPIO_LINE_REVOKE_ALREADY      : u64 = 0xFFFFFF12
  pub let GPIO_LINE_CTRL_LOST           : u64 = 0xFFFFFF11

  // R30.M6-003 (#1077): the last free code in the band. op_arg carries an
  // OPERAND for the level/direction/pull/trigger ops, and everything above
  // that operand's width must be zero. A caller that sets a bit up there is
  // refused, NOT masked, because the fifty-odd bits above the operand are
  // exactly where a pin number would be smuggled and a silent mask would
  // turn an attempt to name a pad into a successful ordinary call.
  pub let GPIO_LINE_OP_ARG_RESERVED     : u64 = 0xFFFFFF10

  pub let GPIO_LINE_DECODE_BAD          : u64 = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF

  // ==========================================================================
  // Cached pad configuration — header bits [55:48], the byte
  // R30.M6-001 reserved and refused to let anything else write.
  //
  // WHY THE ROW CACHES CONFIGURATION AT ALL, given that the pad itself
  // holds the authoritative bits:
  //
  // Because "is this pin an output?" has to be answerable WITHOUT a
  // register read on the refusal path, and because the answer has to mean
  // "this kernel made it an output", not "it reads as one right now". A
  // pad comes out of platform firmware in whatever state firmware left
  // it. Deciding that a SET may proceed because PADCFG0 currently reads
  // as an output is deciding to drive a net on the strength of a
  // configuration nobody in this kernel chose. The cached byte is written
  // ONLY after a successful physical read-modify-write, so
  // GPIO_LINE_CFG_DIR == OUTPUT means this kernel put it there.
  //
  // Layout inside the byte:
  //   [0]    direction   0 = input, 1 = output
  //   [2:1]  pull        0 = none, 1 = down, 2 = up   (3 unused)
  //   [4:3]  trigger     0 = none, 1 = rising, 2 = falling, 3 = both
  //   [5]    subscribed  1 = an edge subscription is live on this line
  //   [7:6]  reserved, must be zero
  //
  // The row is NOT widened: this is the byte #1075 left clear, and +24 is
  // the qword #1075 left clear, and between them they hold everything
  // #1077 and #1078 need.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let GPIO_LINE_CFG_MASK     : u64 = 0x3F
  pub let GPIO_LINE_CFG_DIR_SH   : u64 = 0
  pub let GPIO_LINE_CFG_PULL_SH  : u64 = 1
  pub let GPIO_LINE_CFG_TRIG_SH  : u64 = 3
  pub let GPIO_LINE_CFG_SUB_SH   : u64 = 5

  // ==========================================================================
  // Ops (op_arg[7:0] = op_code) and their required right
  //
  // QUERY ONLY, AND NONE OF THEM TAKES AN OPERAND. `op_arg` is masked to
  // its low byte on entry to the handler, so the fifty-six bits a caller
  // might try to hide a pin number in are discarded before any dispatch
  // decision is made. Ops 0..5 take NO OPERAND at all; the channel ops
  // R30.M6-003 (#1077) adds below take a narrow one and refuse anything
  // above it — see the R30.M6-003 block that follows.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let GPIO_LINE_OP_QUERY_PIN   : u64 = 0   // INVOKE  -> absolute pin
  pub let GPIO_LINE_OP_QUERY_COMM  : u64 = 1   // INVOKE  -> community id
  pub let GPIO_LINE_OP_QUERY_PAD   : u64 = 2   // INVOKE  -> pad index
  pub let GPIO_LINE_OP_QUERY_CTRL  : u64 = 3   // INVOKE  -> parent cap slot
  pub let GPIO_LINE_OP_QUERY_HINT  : u64 = 4   // INVOKE  -> driver hint
  pub let GPIO_LINE_OP_DEBUG_PRINT : u64 = 5   // OBSERVE -> printer

  // ==========================================================================
  // R30.M6-003 (#1077) — THE CHANNEL OPS.
  //
  // Ops 6 and 7 are the two #1075 reserved and left returning
  // INVOKE_UNSUPPORTED to a holder of every right, precisely so that the
  // rights bits would already mean something on the day the ops landed.
  // They land here against THE SAME BITS. A capability minted before this
  // change and carrying only INVOKE still cannot get, set or configure;
  // one minted with READ|WRITE|CONFIG was granted read, write and config
  // authority at mint time and now finds ops behind those bits. Nothing
  // acquires authority it was not minted with, which is what honouring a
  // reservation means.
  //
  //   op  operand           right     effect
  //   --  ----------------  --------  -----------------------------------
  //    6  (none)            READ      sample the line's level
  //    7  level  [8]        WRITE     drive an already-configured output
  //    8  dir    [8]        CONFIG    input <-> output
  //    9  pull   [9:8]      CONFIG    none / down / up
  //   10  trig   [9:8]      CONFIG    edge subscribe (none/rise/fall/both)
  //   11  (none)            CONFIG    edge unsubscribe
  //   12  (none)            OBSERVE   serviced-edge count
  //   13  (none)            INVOKE    cached configuration byte
  //
  // WHY SET IS `WRITE` AND DIRECTION IS `CONFIG`, AND WHY THAT SPLIT IS
  // NOT PEDANTRY: toggling an output changes a voltage on a net this
  // component was granted. Turning an INPUT into an OUTPUT starts DRIVING
  // a net that something else may already be driving — the pad controller
  // will happily source current into another part's low-side driver, and
  // the failure is thermal, not logical, with no status bit anywhere.
  // They are different authorities because their worst outcomes are
  // different in kind.
  //
  // PULL IS `CONFIG` FOR THE SAME REASON AND NOT A WEAKER ONE. A pull-up
  // on a line an external device drives low is a fight: a real current
  // path from the rail through the pad's termination resistor into
  // whatever is holding the net down. It is a weaker fight than a driver
  // conflict and it is the same category of fight. A "preference" that
  // can put a resistor across a live net is configuration authority.
  //
  // OPERANDS AND CONFINEMENT. These ops take operands, which ops 0..5
  // deliberately did not, so the op_arg discipline changes shape rather
  // than relaxing: op_arg[7:0] is the op, op_arg[15:8] is the operand,
  // and op_arg[63:16] MUST BE ZERO or the invocation is refused
  // GPIO_LINE_OP_ARG_RESERVED. Each op then masks the operand to its own
  // width (one or two bits) and REFUSES an out-of-range value rather than
  // truncating it. An eight-bit operand cannot express a pin number on a
  // 512-pin part, cannot express a community, and cannot express a pad
  // index; and the fifty-six bits where one WOULD fit are a refusal, not
  // a mask. The pad address still comes from gpio_line_pad_off_of_slot
  // and from nowhere else.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let GPIO_LINE_OP_GET         : u64 = 6   // READ    -> level 0|1
  pub let GPIO_LINE_OP_SET         : u64 = 7   // WRITE   <- level
  pub let GPIO_LINE_OP_CFG_DIR     : u64 = 8   // CONFIG  <- direction
  pub let GPIO_LINE_OP_CFG_PULL    : u64 = 9   // CONFIG  <- pull
  pub let GPIO_LINE_OP_EDGE_SUB    : u64 = 10  // CONFIG  <- trigger
  pub let GPIO_LINE_OP_EDGE_UNSUB  : u64 = 11  // CONFIG
  pub let GPIO_LINE_OP_EDGE_COUNT  : u64 = 12  // OBSERVE -> serviced edges
  pub let GPIO_LINE_OP_QUERY_CFG   : u64 = 13  // INVOKE  -> cfg byte

  pub let GPIO_LINE_OP_MAX         : u64 = 13

  // ==========================================================================
  // Backing table (.bss, align 64) — MODULE-PRIVATE BY CONSTRUCTION.
  //
  // 32 rows * 32 bytes = 1024 bytes = 128 u64s. .bss zero-init means
  // every row starts in_use = 0, the free sentinel the allocator scans
  // for.
  //
  // THIS IS THE ONLY PLACE IN THE KERNEL WHERE A GPIO PIN ASSIGNMENT
  // LIVES, and tools/build.sh asserts it with `objdump -r`.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let mut _gpio_line_table : [u64; 128] = uninit @align(64)

  // ==========================================================================
  // Canonical debug-printer strings (.rodata)
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let gpl_dbg_msg : [u8; 14] = "GPIO LINE ROW\0"
  pub let gpl_k_row   : [u8; 4]  = "row\0"
  pub let gpl_k_ctl   : [u8; 4]  = "ctl\0"
  pub let gpl_k_pin   : [u8; 4]  = "pin\0"
  pub let gpl_k_com   : [u8; 4]  = "com\0"
  pub let gpl_k_pad   : [u8; 4]  = "pad\0"

  // ==========================================================================
  // gpio_line_table_reset() -> ()
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let gpio_line_table_reset : () -> () !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M6-001 (#1075): zero all 128 u64s of _gpio_line_table, returning every row to the free state the .bss zero-init established. Exists so the boot witness can start from a known table and so a failing witness cannot poison a later one -- the posture acpi_evt_table_reset established in #1068 and i2c_slave_table_reset in #1071. This is a table reset, not a revoke: it does not touch any descriptor, and it is called only by fixture setup where the capability slots are being cleared in the same breath. Loop over 128 qwords. Leaf. Reserved labels: gpl_tr_.",
      block: {
        lea r8, [rip + _gpio_line_table];
        xor rax, rax;
        xor rcx, rcx;

      gpl_tr_loop:
        cmp rcx, 128;
        jae gpl_tr_done;
        mov [r8], rax;
        add r8, 8;
        add rcx, 1;
        jmp gpl_tr_loop;

      gpl_tr_done:
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // gpio_line_rights_valid(rights) -> u64
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let gpio_line_rights_valid : (u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (rights: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M6-001 (#1075): 1 iff (rights & R_GPIO_LINE_ALL) == rights. R_GPIO_LINE_ALL = 0x41F (READ|WRITE|CONFIG|INVOKE|REVOKE|OBSERVE). A subset test, so a capability that may read a pin's level but never drive it is expressible, and so is one that may drive it but never change its direction. RIGHT_MINT (0x200) is OUTSIDE the mask: this kind is the bottom of the derivation lattice and a MINT bit no gate reads is the shape that later acquires a meaning nobody audited. READ, WRITE and CONFIG are inside the mask although nothing in this milestone consumes them -- reserving them now is what keeps an already-minted capability's authority STABLE on the day #1077 and #1078 add the ops, instead of silently widening it. Pure leaf. Reserved labels: gpl_rv_.",
      block: {
        mov rax, rdi;
        mov r10, 0x41F;
        and rax, r10;
        cmp rax, rdi;
        jne gpl_rv_no;
        mov rax, 1;
        ret;

      gpl_rv_no:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // gpio_line_check_parent_device(parent_slot) -> u64
  //
  // THE FIRST HALF OF THE DERIVATION GATE. 1 iff cap_table[parent_slot]
  // is a KIND_DEVICE carrying RIGHT_MINT.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let gpio_line_check_parent_device : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{cap} =
    fn (parent_slot: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {cap},
      justification: "R30.M6-001 (#1075): the first half of the KIND_GPIO_LINE derivation gate. Bounds-check parent_slot < 256; load the descriptor at cap_table + parent_slot*24 (stride 24 per cap/mint.pdx); require kind == KIND_DEVICE (10) AND (rights & RIGHT_MINT 0x200) != 0. This half alone is deliberately NOT sufficient and the mint does not treat it as such: every PCI function in the machine is a KIND_DEVICE, so a gate that stopped here would let the holder of any device capability mint pins -- except that it could not say WHICH controller, because the identity is inherited rather than argued. The second half, in gpio_line_cap_mint_inner, is that the inherited identity must resolve to a probed, identified pad controller; a KIND_DEVICE for the network card reaches it and is refused GPIO_LINE_MINT_NO_CONTROLLER. Pure read. Reserved labels: gpl_cpd_.",
      block: {
        mov r11, 256;
        cmp rdi, r11;
        jae gpl_cpd_no;

        mov rax, cap_table;
        mov r8, rdi;
        shl r8, 3;
        mov r9, rdi;
        shl r9, 4;
        add r8, r9;
        add rax, r8;

        mov r10, [rax + 0];
        cmp r10, 10;                     // KIND_DEVICE
        jne gpl_cpd_no;

        mov r10, [rax + 8];
        mov r11, 0x200;                  // RIGHT_MINT
        and r10, r11;
        cmp r10, 0;
        je gpl_cpd_no;

        mov rax, 1;
        ret;

      gpl_cpd_no:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // gpio_line_parent_ctrl_id(parent_slot) -> controller_id | 0
  //
  // CONTROLLER IDENTITY IS INHERITED, NEVER ARGUED.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let gpio_line_parent_ctrl_id : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{cap} =
    fn (parent_slot: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {cap},
      justification: "R30.M6-001 (#1075): read the parent KIND_DEVICE descriptor's target_ptr, which for a device capability is its bdf_pack key. This is the ONLY source of a line capability's controller identity: the mint has no controller argument, so the pin it authorises is on the device its parent capability names and on no other. That is the discipline #1070 established for KIND_I2C_BUS, and it is what makes the second half of the derivation gate possible -- the identity that gets looked up in the controller table is one the caller could not choose. Returns 0 for an out-of-range slot or a non-KIND_DEVICE descriptor; 0 is also what lpss_gpio_find_by_ctrl_id refuses outright, so a fabricated parent cannot resolve to controller index 0 by accident. Pure read. Reserved labels: gpl_pci_.",
      block: {
        mov r11, 256;
        cmp rdi, r11;
        jae gpl_pci_no;

        mov rax, cap_table;
        mov r8, rdi;
        shl r8, 3;
        mov r9, rdi;
        shl r9, 4;
        add r8, r9;
        add rax, r8;

        mov r10, [rax + 0];
        cmp r10, 10;                     // KIND_DEVICE
        jne gpl_pci_no;

        mov rax, [rax + 16];             // target_ptr = bdf key
        ret;

      gpl_pci_no:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // gpio_line_tail_pack(parent_slot, pin, pad_index, community)
  //   -> hdr | GPIO_LINE_DECODE_BAD
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let gpio_line_tail_pack : (u64, u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (parent_slot: u64) (pin: u64) (pad_index: u64) (community: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M6-001 (#1075): canonical KIND_GPIO_LINE header-word encoder. Validates parent_slot < 256, pin < GPIO_PIN_MAX (512), pad_index <= pin (a community-relative index can never EXCEED the absolute pin it was derived from, because pad_index = pin - pin_base and pin_base >= 0 -- so a header claiming otherwise is one nobody could have produced from a real mapping) and community <= 0xFF. The pad_index <= pin rule is why this is an encoder and not four shifts at the call site: it is a CROSS-FIELD invariant, and a row carrying a pad index inconsistent with its pin would be a capability whose recorded pin and recorded register address disagree -- every consumer would then have to decide independently which of the two to believe. Making the combination unrepresentable is cheaper than agreeing on a repair. Pack: parent_slot | (pin << 8) | (pad_index << 24) | (community << 40). Bits [55:48] left clear as reserved for #1078's configuration and [63:56] left clear for the allocator's in_use flag. Five pushes (rbx, r12-r15) hold the four fields; no nested calls. Reserved labels: gpl_tp_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        push r15;

        mov r12, rdi;                    // parent_slot
        mov r13, rsi;                    // pin
        mov r14, rdx;                    // pad_index
        mov r15, rcx;                    // community

        mov rbx, 256;
        cmp r12, rbx;
        jae gpl_tp_bad;
        mov rbx, 512;                    // GPIO_PIN_MAX
        cmp r13, rbx;
        jae gpl_tp_bad;
        cmp r14, r13;
        ja  gpl_tp_bad;
        mov rbx, 0xFF;
        cmp r15, rbx;
        ja  gpl_tp_bad;

        mov rax, r12;
        and rax, 0xFF;                   // parent_slot

        mov rbx, r13;
        mov r10, 0xFFFF;
        and rbx, r10;
        shl rbx, 8;
        or  rax, rbx;                    // | pin << 8

        mov rbx, r14;
        mov r10, 0xFFFF;
        and rbx, r10;
        shl rbx, 24;
        or  rax, rbx;                    // | pad_index << 24

        mov rbx, r15;
        and rbx, 0xFF;
        shl rbx, 40;
        or  rax, rbx;                    // | community << 40

        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret;

      gpl_tp_bad:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // Header-word field extractors — the decode half of the round-trip.
  // These operate on the WORD, not on a row.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let gpio_line_hdr_parent : (u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (hdr: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M6-001 (#1075): parent_slot from header bits [7:0]. Pure leaf.",
      block: {
        mov rax, rdi;
        and rax, 0xFF;
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let gpio_line_hdr_pin : (u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (hdr: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M6-001 (#1075): pin from header bits [23:8]. Pure leaf. Takes a header WORD, never a caller-supplied pin -- this is a decoder, and the only headers it is ever handed come from live rows.",
      block: {
        mov rax, rdi;
        shr rax, 8;
        mov r10, 0xFFFF;
        and rax, r10;
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let gpio_line_hdr_pad : (u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (hdr: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M6-001 (#1075): pad_index from header bits [39:24]. Pure leaf.",
      block: {
        mov rax, rdi;
        shr rax, 24;
        mov r10, 0xFFFF;
        and rax, r10;
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let gpio_line_hdr_community : (u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (hdr: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M6-001 (#1075): community from header bits [47:40]. Pure leaf.",
      block: {
        mov rax, rdi;
        shr rax, 40;
        and rax, 0xFF;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // gpio_line_tail_alloc(hdr, controller_id, driver_hint) -> row_id | error
  //
  // THE ONLY WRITER OF _gpio_line_table.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let gpio_line_tail_alloc : (u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (hdr: u64) (controller_id: u64) (driver_hint: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M6-001 (#1075): allocate and populate a free _gpio_line_table row. THIS IS THE ONLY FUNCTION IN THE KERNEL THAT WRITES A GPIO PIN ASSIGNMENT, which is what makes the derivation gate and the pin policy upstream of it exhaustive rather than merely usual. Gates, in order: hdr bits [63:56] non-zero -> GPIO_LINE_TAIL_BAD_ARG (those bits are the allocator's in_use flag; a caller that pre-set them would be handed a row the free scan skips); hdr reserved bits [55:48] non-zero -> GPIO_LINE_TAIL_BAD_ARG (a reserved bit that is merely ignored is a reserved bit #1078 cannot use for direction and pull); the header's own pin re-checked against GPIO_PIN_MAX and its pad_index against its pin -> GPIO_LINE_TAIL_BAD_ARG, so even a fabricated header cannot land an inconsistent pin/pad pair in a row; controller_id == 0 -> GPIO_LINE_TAIL_BAD_ARG, because zero is what lpss_gpio_find_by_ctrl_id treats as 'no controller' and a row carrying it would be permanently unresolvable; driver_hint == 0 -> GPIO_LINE_TAIL_BAD_ARG, because zero is the 'unclaimed' sentinel the R31 binder reads and a capability that is both minted and unclaimed is a state with no meaning. Low-first scan over 32 rows of 32 bytes; free iff the in_use byte (byte 7 of [row+0]) is zero; exhaustion -> GPIO_LINE_TAIL_ENOSPC. Claim: [row+0] = hdr | (1 << 56), [row+8] = controller_id, [row+16] = driver_hint, [row+24] = 0. Leaf, no nested calls. Reserved labels: gpl_ta_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        push r12;
        push r13;

        mov r12, rdi;                    // hdr
        mov r13, rsi;                    // controller_id
        mov rbx, rdx;                    // driver_hint

        // Gate: hdr bits [63:56] must be zero.
        mov r10, r12;
        shr r10, 56;
        cmp r10, 0;
        jne gpl_ta_bad_arg;

        // Gate: hdr reserved bits [55:48] must be zero.
        mov r10, r12;
        shr r10, 48;
        and r10, 0xFF;
        cmp r10, 0;
        jne gpl_ta_bad_arg;

        // Gate: controller identity present.
        cmp r13, 0;
        je gpl_ta_bad_arg;

        // Gate: driver hint present.
        cmp rbx, 0;
        je gpl_ta_bad_arg;

        // Gate: the header's own pin is representable and its pad index
        // is consistent with it.
        mov r10, r12;
        shr r10, 8;
        mov r11, 0xFFFF;
        and r10, r11;                    // pin
        mov r9, 512;                     // GPIO_PIN_MAX
        cmp r10, r9;
        jae gpl_ta_bad_arg;
        mov r9, r12;
        shr r9, 24;
        and r9, r11;                     // pad_index
        cmp r9, r10;
        ja gpl_ta_bad_arg;

        // Row cursor.
        lea r8, [rip + _gpio_line_table];
        xor rcx, rcx;

      gpl_ta_scan:
        cmp rcx, 32;                     // GPIO_LINE_MAX
        jae gpl_ta_none;
        mov rax, [r8];
        mov r10, rax;
        shr r10, 56;                     // in_use byte
        cmp r10, 0;
        je gpl_ta_found;
        add r8, 32;                      // GPIO_LINE_ROW_BYTES
        add rcx, 1;
        jmp gpl_ta_scan;

      gpl_ta_found:
        mov rax, r12;
        mov r10, 1;
        shl r10, 56;
        or  rax, r10;
        mov [r8 + 0], rax;               // hdr | in_use

        mov [r8 + 8],  r13;              // controller_id
        mov [r8 + 16], rbx;              // driver_hint

        xor rax, rax;
        mov [r8 + 24], rax;              // reserved

        mov rax, rcx;                    // row_id
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret;

      gpl_ta_none:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFF1F;             // GPIO_LINE_TAIL_ENOSPC
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret;

      gpl_ta_bad_arg:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFF1E;             // GPIO_LINE_TAIL_BAD_ARG
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // gpio_line_tail_free(row_id) -> ()
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let gpio_line_tail_free : (u64) -> () !{mem} @{} =
    fn (row_id: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M6-001 (#1075): row_id >= GPIO_LINE_MAX -> silent no-op. Otherwise zero all four u64s of the row. Zeroing [row+0] clears in_use, so free and SCRUB are the same operation. Scrubbing matters here for a reason specific to this kind: (controller_id, pin) is the uniqueness key, so a freed-but-unscrubbed row would keep refusing the next legitimate mint of that pin with GPIO_LINE_MINT_PIN_IN_USE -- a ghost owner that no capability names and no driver can displace, on a pin that may be the only way to bring a device out of reset. Leaf. Reserved labels: gpl_tf_.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 32;
        jae gpl_tf_done;

        mov r8, rdi;
        shl r8, 5;                       // row_id * 32
        lea r9, [rip + _gpio_line_table];
        add r8, r9;

        xor rax, rax;
        mov [r8 + 0],  rax;
        mov [r8 + 8],  rax;
        mov [r8 + 16], rax;
        mov [r8 + 24], rax;

      gpl_tf_done:
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // gpio_line_tail_valid(row_id) -> u64
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let gpio_line_tail_valid : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (row_id: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M6-001 (#1075): 1 iff row_id < GPIO_LINE_MAX and the row's in_use byte is non-zero. Same shape as opregion_tail_valid and i2c_slave_tail_valid. Leaf, pure read. Reserved labels: gpl_tv_.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 32;
        jae gpl_tv_no;

        mov r8, rdi;
        shl r8, 5;
        lea r9, [rip + _gpio_line_table];
        add r8, r9;

        mov rax, [r8 + 0];
        shr rax, 56;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je gpl_tv_no;

        mov rax, 1;
        ret;

      gpl_tv_no:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // gpio_line_rows_live() -> count
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let gpio_line_rows_live : () -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M6-001 (#1075): number of _gpio_line_table rows whose in_use byte is set. THE standing observable for row leakage, and the assertion the cascade is checked against: a controller teardown that returns this to its pre-mint value has leaked nothing, and phase 2 of the cascade exists precisely so it always does. Kept as a plain exported query rather than a capability op because it is a property of the TABLE, and putting it behind a capability would let the holder of one pin's capability measure how many other pins are claimed. Leaf, pure read. Reserved labels: gpl_rl_.",
      block: {
        lea r8, [rip + _gpio_line_table];
        xor rcx, rcx;
        xor rax, rax;

      gpl_rl_scan:
        cmp rcx, 32;
        jae gpl_rl_done;
        mov r10, [r8 + 0];
        shr r10, 56;
        cmp r10, 0;
        je gpl_rl_next;
        add rax, 1;

      gpl_rl_next:
        add r8, 32;
        add rcx, 1;
        jmp gpl_rl_scan;

      gpl_rl_done:
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // Row decoders. Every one bounds-checks and in_use-checks first, and
  // returns GPIO_LINE_DECODE_BAD on a dead or out-of-range row.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let gpio_line_row_hdr : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (row_id: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M6-001 (#1075): raw header word of a LIVE row with the in_use flag stripped, so the value round-trips against gpio_line_tail_pack's output; GPIO_LINE_DECODE_BAD otherwise. The other decoders are written in terms of this one so the bounds check and the liveness check exist once and cannot drift apart. Leaf, pure read. Reserved labels: gpl_rh_.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 32;
        jae gpl_rh_bad;

        mov r8, rdi;
        shl r8, 5;
        lea r9, [rip + _gpio_line_table];
        add r8, r9;

        mov rax, [r8 + 0];
        mov r10, rax;
        shr r10, 56;
        cmp r10, 0;
        je gpl_rh_bad;

        mov r10, 0x00FFFFFFFFFFFFFF;
        and rax, r10;
        ret;

      gpl_rh_bad:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;
        ret
      }
    }

  // -- THE PIN RESOLVER, ROW FORM. ARITY ONE, AND tools/build.sh PINS
  //    IT. There is no second parameter for a caller to pass a pin in;
  //    the pin is read out of the row named by row_id and nowhere else.
  pub let gpio_line_row_pin : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (row_id: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M6-001 (#1075): THE ABSOLUTE PIN OF A LINE CAPABILITY, read from header bits [23:8] of a live row. GPIO_LINE_DECODE_BAD on a dead or out-of-range row -- never a plausible zero, which matters because pin 0 is a real, drivable pin on every pad controller and a decoder that returned it for a dead row would be handing back authority over community 0's first pad. ARITY IS ONE AND IT IS LOAD-BEARING: there is no parameter through which a caller could supply a pin, which is what makes 'act on a pin other than the one my capability names' unphrasable rather than merely refused. tools/build.sh asserts the literal signature line of this function and of the four capability-form resolvers, so a mutant that adds a second parameter fails the build. One push (rbx, unused) aligns the nested call. Reserved labels: gpl_rp_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        call gpio_line_row_hdr;
        mov r10, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;
        cmp rax, r10;
        je gpl_rp_out;
        shr rax, 8;
        mov r10, 0xFFFF;
        and rax, r10;
      gpl_rp_out:
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let gpio_line_row_pad : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (row_id: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M6-001 (#1075): community-relative pad index of a live row, from header bits [39:24]. GPIO_LINE_DECODE_BAD on a dead row. Stored rather than recomputed from the pin on every access, because recomputing means consulting the community table again and a table that changed between mint and use would move the capability's pad -- the row is what the platform decided, and it does not move. One push (rbx, unused) aligns the nested call. Reserved labels: gpl_rpd_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        call gpio_line_row_hdr;
        mov r10, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;
        cmp rax, r10;
        je gpl_rpd_out;
        shr rax, 24;
        mov r10, 0xFFFF;
        and rax, r10;
      gpl_rpd_out:
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let gpio_line_row_community : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (row_id: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M6-001 (#1075): community id of a live row, from header bits [47:40]. GPIO_LINE_DECODE_BAD on a dead row. Carried in the row rather than re-derived from the pin for the same reason the pad index is: the community selects the register WINDOW, so a community that differed from the one the mint validated would put every access on a different bank. One push (rbx, unused) aligns the nested call. Reserved labels: gpl_rc_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        call gpio_line_row_hdr;
        mov r10, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;
        cmp rax, r10;
        je gpl_rc_out;
        shr rax, 40;
        and rax, 0xFF;
      gpl_rc_out:
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let gpio_line_row_parent : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (row_id: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R30.M6-001 (#1075): parent KIND_DEVICE cap slot of a live row, from header bits [7:0]. Recorded for audit and query only; the CASCADE key is controller_id, not this, because a cap slot can be reused by a different device while a bdf key names the part. GPIO_LINE_DECODE_BAD on a dead row. One push (rbx, unused) aligns the nested call. Reserved labels: gpl_rpa_.",
